  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Arkansas received $977M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 14,892 awards with an average award size of $65.6K. Obligations fell 22.7% year over year. Recent fiscal history shows fluctuations: FY21 totaled $1.5B, FY22 totaled $1.4B, FY23 totaled $1.3B, FY24 totaled $1.9B, FY25 totaled $1.3B, and FY26 totals $977M through the fiscal year to date. The Department of Defense accounted for the largest share of buying, obligating $564M, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs at $159M and the General Services Administration at $57.3M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry by obligation at $226M, followed by Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $121M and Ammunition (except Small Arms) Manufacturing at $63.2M. BL HARBERT INTERNATIONAL LLC is the top vendor with $102M, followed by ALBERICI CONSTRUCTORS INC at $68.2M and GENERAL DYNAMICS-OTS, INC. at $63.2M.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed in Arkansas federal contracting compared with prior years?",
        "body": "Yearly obligations moved up and down: FY21 was $1.5B, FY22 was $1.4B, FY23 was $1.3B, FY24 was $1.9B, FY25 was $1.3B, and FY26 is $977M to date. Obligations fell 22.7% year over year. Award volume remains substantial with 14,892 awards and an average award size of $65.6K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries drive federal spending in Arkansas?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $564M, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs at $159M and the General Services Administration at $57.3M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction leads industries with $226M, Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction follows at $121M, and Ammunition (except Small Arms) Manufacturing is $63.2M."
      }
    ]
